VALUES ('6bc1a62b-fdab-52a8-90f7-5642ea45d19f', '57f5e0f7-67b7-55ab-a3e7-469947105cd5', 'dbf6bdc0-1816-51f5-873f-a154ad52b4a6', 'Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)', 'notes', 14, $md$Picture a party where you keep introducing people. Every time you introduce two strangers, their two friend circles merge into one. At any moment, you might ask "are these two people in the same friend circle now?" Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union, or DSU) answers exactly that question, and merges groups, both in near-constant time. It replaces slower BFS/DFS connectivity checks, and it's the difference between a working solution and a timeout on problems where connections arrive one at a time, like Number of Islands II.

## Find: walking up to the group's leader, with a shortcut

`find(x)` walks up parent pointers from `x` until it reaches the root of `x`'s tree, a node that is its own parent. That root acts as the "name" of the whole group. Without any optimization, this walk can take O(n) steps on a long, chain-like tree. Path compression fixes that: while walking up to find the root, rewire every node you pass through to point directly at the root. The next `find` call through any of those nodes is then instant.

```python
def find(self, x: int) -> int:
    if self.parent[x] != x:
        self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])   # path compression
    return self.parent[x]
```

```javascript +
function find(x) {
    if (this.parent[x] !== x) {
        this.parent[x] = find.call(this, this.parent[x]); // path compression
    }
    return this.parent[x];
}
```

```java +
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DSU dsu = new DSU(new int[]{0, 0, 1, 3});
        System.out.println(dsu.find(3));
    }
}

class DSU {
    int[] parent;

    DSU(int[] parent) {
        this.parent = parent;
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = find(parent[x]); // path compression
        }
        return parent[x];
    }
}
```

This looks like a simple recursive one-liner, but it does real work: the recursive call returns the root, and the assignment flattens `x`'s pointer to point straight at that root, permanently shortening the path for every future call that passes through `x`.

> **Remember:** find() walks up to the root. Path compression rewires every node it passes through to point directly at that root, so the next lookup is instant.

```knowledge-check
