```

## Union by rank: keep the merged tree shallow

`union(x, y)` merges the two groups containing `x` and `y` by attaching one tree's root under the other's. Attaching them arbitrarily can, over many merges, build a tall, unbalanced tree. Union by rank (or by size) always attaches the shorter tree under the taller one's root, keeping the whole structure shallow.

```python
def union(self, x: int, y: int) -> None:
    root_x, root_y = self.find(x), self.find(y)
    if root_x == root_y:
        return   # already connected
    if self.rank[root_x] < self.rank[root_y]:
        root_x, root_y = root_y, root_x
    self.parent[root_y] = root_x
    if self.rank[root_x] == self.rank[root_y]:
        self.rank[root_x] += 1
```

```javascript +
function union(x, y) {
    let rootX = find.call(this, x);
    let rootY = find.call(this, y);
    if (rootX === rootY) {
        return; // already connected
    }
    if (this.rank[rootX] < this.rank[rootY]) {
        [rootX, rootY] = [rootY, rootX];
    }
    this.parent[rootY] = rootX;
    if (this.rank[rootX] === this.rank[rootY]) {
        this.rank[rootX]++;
    }
}
```

```java +
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DSU dsu = new DSU(4);
        dsu.union(0, 1);
        dsu.union(2, 3);
        System.out.println(dsu.find(1) == dsu.find(0));
    }
}

class DSU {
    int[] parent;
    int[] rank;

    DSU(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        }
        return parent[x];
    }

    void union(int x, int y) {
        int rootX = find(x);
        int rootY = find(y);
        if (rootX == rootY) {
            return; // already connected
        }
        if (rank[rootX] < rank[rootY]) {
            int tmp = rootX;
            rootX = rootY;
            rootY = tmp;
        }
        parent[rootY] = rootX;
        if (rank[rootX] == rank[rootY]) {
            rank[rootX]++;
        }
    }
}
```

Combined, path compression and union by rank give an amortized time of O(α(n)) per operation, where α is the inverse Ackermann function. For any `n` you could realistically encounter, α(n) is at most 4, which is why this is described as "nearly O(1)."

> **Remember:** always attach the shorter tree under the taller one's root. Combined with path compression, this makes every operation nearly O(1).

```knowledge-check
{ "questions": [
    { "id": "dsa-union-find-union-rank-q1", "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "Why does union by rank attach the shorter tree under the taller tree's root, rather than the other way around?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "It keeps the overall tree shallow, since the taller tree's height doesn't grow when a shorter tree is added underneath it"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "It's required to make find() return the correct root"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "It reduces memory usage"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "It has no real effect; the choice is arbitrary"}
      ],
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "If you always attach a shorter tree under a taller one, the taller tree's height stays the same. Attaching the taller tree under the shorter one instead would increase the overall height, degrading future find() calls." }
] }
```

## Using union-find to catch a cycle as you build a graph

DSU gives a clean way to detect a cycle while building a graph one edge at a time: before adding an edge `(u, v)`, check whether `find(u) == find(v)`. If they're already in the same group, this edge would create a cycle, so either skip it, or, if you add it anyway, you've just confirmed a cycle exists. This is exactly how Kruskal's minimum-spanning-tree algorithm decides which edges to keep, and it's the core trick behind Graph Valid Tree below.

```python
def has_cycle_on_add(self, u: int, v: int) -> bool:
    if self.find(u) == self.find(v):
        return True   # adding this edge would close a cycle
    self.union(u, v)
    return False
```

```javascript +
function hasCycleOnAdd(u, v) {
    if (find.call(this, u) === find.call(this, v)) {
        return true; // adding this edge would close a cycle
    }
    union.call(this, u, v);
    return false;
}
```

```java +
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DSU dsu = new DSU(4);
        System.out.println(dsu.hasCycleOnAdd(0, 1));
        System.out.println(dsu.hasCycleOnAdd(1, 0));
    }
}

class DSU {
    int[] parent;
    int[] rank;

    DSU(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        }
        return parent[x];
    }

    void union(int x, int y) {
        int rootX = find(x);
        int rootY = find(y);
        if (rootX == rootY) return;
        if (rank[rootX] < rank[rootY]) {
            int tmp = rootX;
            rootX = rootY;
            rootY = tmp;
        }
        parent[rootY] = rootX;
        if (rank[rootX] == rank[rootY]) {
            rank[rootX]++;
        }
    }

    boolean hasCycleOnAdd(int u, int v) {
        if (find(u) == find(v)) {
            return true; // adding this edge would close a cycle
        }
        union(u, v);
        return false;
    }
}
```

> **Remember:** if find(u) equals find(v) before you union them, adding that edge would close a cycle.

```knowledge-check
{ "questions": [
    { "id": "dsa-union-find-cycle-q1", "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "How does union-find detect that adding an edge (u, v) would create a cycle?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "If find(u) already equals find(v), both nodes are already in the same group, so the new edge would close a loop"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "It counts the total number of edges added so far"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "It runs a full BFS after every edge to check for cycles"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "It checks whether u and v have the same rank"}
      ],
