      "prompt": "Why is union-find a better fit than re-running BFS/DFS after every new land cell in Number of Islands II?",
      "options": [
        {"id": "a", "text": "Connectivity is being queried incrementally between additions, and DSU processes each addition in near-constant time instead of O(n) per query"},
        {"id": "b", "text": "BFS/DFS cannot correctly count islands on a grid at all"},
        {"id": "c", "text": "DSU uses less memory than BFS/DFS in every case"},
        {"id": "d", "text": "The problem requires a sorted order of results, which only DSU can produce"}
      ],
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "Re-running BFS/DFS from scratch after every single addition costs O(n) each time, O(n*k) total for k additions. DSU updates the existing structure incrementally, near O(1) amortized per addition, which is exactly the situation DSU is built for." }
] }
```

The four problems in this lesson split along one axis: is the graph built once (Number of Connected Components, Graph Valid Tree), or does it grow incrementally (Longest Consecutive Sequence's value-by-value unions, Number of Islands II's cell-by-cell additions)? For the one-time case, BFS/DFS is usually just as good as DSU, and sometimes simpler to write. DSU earns its place specifically when connectivity needs to be checked *between* additions, not only once at the very end.
