## When greedy fails

Signs a problem is not greedy-safe:

- The "obviously best" local choice depends on information you don't have yet (later constraints change what "best" even means).
- A counterexample is easy to build by hand; always try 2-3 small cases before committing to greedy.
- The problem asks for a count of *all* ways, or needs backtracking over choices: greedy produces one answer, not an exploration of alternatives.

If you can't rule these out quickly, say so out loud in the interview and default to DP. Proposing greedy and then getting handed the counterexample mid-interview is a worse outcome than never proposing it at all.

> **Remember:** if you can build a counterexample in under a minute, greedy is wrong for this problem. Test small cases before you commit.

